    48	needs to not fight the focus. Python gets out of the way.
    49	
    50	This also means the system is trivially portable. Everything is immutable,
    51	append-only, unidirectional. There's no mutable state to coordinate, no
    52	concurrency model baked in, no framework coupling. The Python is one
    53	instantiation. The paradigm is what matters.
    54	
    55	## Libraries
    56	
    57	Eight libraries in `libs/`. Each owns one concern. The runtime dependency graph is
    58	`_LIB_ALLOWED_RUNTIME` in `tests/test_architecture.py` — everything not listed
    59	there is forbidden, and the test is the authority:
    60	
    61	```
    62	atoms    ──→ ()
    63	lang     ──→ ()
    64	sign     ──→ ()
    65	engine   ──→ lang, atoms
    66	store    ──→ engine
    67	custody  ──→ sign, engine
    68	sdk      ──→ atoms, lang, sign, engine, store, custody
    69	migrate  ──→ engine, lang
    70	```
    71	
    72	`X ──→ Y` reads *X imports Y at runtime*. painted is not in this graph — it
    73	lives in its own repo and arrives as a versioned PyPI dependency (see below).
    74	
    75	**atoms** — The ingress shapes as code. Fact and Spec as frozen dataclasses; the
    76	third shape, Tick, lives in engine, because a Tick is a runtime output rather
    77	than something you configure. Source as ingress configuration. Parse vocabulary (11 ops: Split, Pick, Rename, Coerce...) for
    78	shaping raw input. Fold vocabulary (10 ops: Latest, Count, Sum, Collect,
    79	Upsert...) for accumulating state. `Spec.apply(state, payload) → state` is pure
    80	— deep copies, folds, returns. Zero external dependencies, stdlib only
    81	(enforced by Rule 6).
    82	
    83	**engine** — The pattern as code. Vertex routes facts by kind to fold engines.
    84	Loop executes Spec.apply, tracks state, fires at boundaries. SqliteStore provides
    85	durable append-only persistence, the tick hash chain, and the signing injection
    86	points (`tick_signer`, `fact_signer`, `verify_chain`/`verify_facts` — callables
    87	in, never an import of the crypto). StoreReader provides read-only inspection.
    88	Peer/Grant provides identity and gating policy (implemented; no in-repo consumer
    89	yet — vouch is the forcing function). The compiler translates lang AST into
    90	runtime vertices. Engine imports lang at module scope for AST types and atoms
    91	through function-local lazy imports.
    92	
    93	**lang** — Configuration as code. KDL parser for `.loop` files (source
    94	definitions) and `.vertex` files (vertex configurations). Pure grammar — no
    95	runtime types, no execution. Produces frozen AST dataclasses. Validates shape
    96	inference through parse pipelines. The only external dependency is `ckdl`.
    97	
    98	**store** — Maintenance as code. Slice, merge, receive, compact, transport
    99	(push/pull), and rebirth for vertex store databases. Operates on the same SQLite
   100	databases that engine writes; it imports engine for `tick_row_hash` so chain
   101	hashing stays single-sourced. ULID primary keys make cross-database dedup trivial
   102	— same fact in two stores has the same ID, merge is `INSERT OR IGNORE`. Search is
   103	*not* here: FTS5 lives behind engine's vertex read interface.
   104	
   105	**sign** — Cryptographic primitives as code. RSA `KeyStore` with
   106	`load_or_generate`, JWT `mint`/`verify`, JWKS and OpenID-configuration document
   107	building, and `sign.ed25519` for detached digest signatures with domain
   108	separation. Deliberately loops-agnostic — it knows nothing of Fact, Tick, or
   109	vertices, which is what lets vouch, pile, and comms share it. Depends on
   110	`cryptography`, `pyjwt`, `python-ulid`; no internal loops deps.
   111	
   112	**custody** — Signing composition as code. One module, and the place where sign
   113	meets engine's injection points. Owns the domain-separation constants
   114	`loops-tick-v1`/`loops-fact-v1` (string-pinned here by Rule 8 — import them,
   115	never re-hardcode); the `keys/` custody layout co-located with the store it
   116	signs; `ensure_signing_key` as the single minting entry point; and the
   117	signer/verifier builders engine's callables expect. Engine never imports custody
   118	— injection, not import. Apps compose the two.
   119	
   120	**sdk** — Apex headless composition as code. The single composition point that
   121	unites atoms, store, lang, sign, custody, and engine into typed, headless
   122	operations (`declare`, `target`, `read`, `emit`, `kind`). Presentation surfaces
   123	(CLI commands, TUI shells, automation agents) consume `sdk` exclusively and contain
   124	zero substrate orchestration logic.
   125	
   126	**migrate** — The one-way door as code. The migration sidecar: frozen copies of
   127	the legacy formats (`legacy_jsonl`, `legacy_sqlite`, `legacy_ids`), the
   128	validated row stream and inventory pass with the GF-3 refusal family, the
   129	transformer to arrival drafts, and the sink (mint → append → verify → signed
   130	report → atomic descriptor publish). Quarantined by construction: nothing
   131	imports migrate, and migrate imports no legacy runtime modules (AST ratchet in
   132	its own tests until slice 5 deletes those modules). Signers are injected —
   133	migrate never imports custody or sign. Slated to shrink, not grow: legacy
   134	knowledge lives here so the runtime can drop it.
   135	
   136	**painted** — Lenses as code, and no longer in this repo. Terminal rendering
   137	primitives (Cell, Span, Line, Block, Style), composition (join, pad, border,
   138	truncate), and the `run_cli` harness that wires zoom levels and output modes into
   139	a standard CLI pattern. Lenses are functions: `(data, zoom, width) → Block`. It
   140	lives at [painted](https://github.com/kgruel/painted) and is consumed as a
   141	versioned PyPI dependency, pinned in the root `pyproject.toml`. Rendering changes
   142	happen upstream, not here.
   143	
   144	## Layers — the instrument families
   145	
   146	The libraries group into layers by **role** — which part of the communication
   147	problem each serves. The mapping follows Weaver's three levels of the
   148	communication problem (instrument-family survey, 2026-07-26; see
   149	`design:architecture/surfacing-layer-charter` in the project store):
   150	
   151	| Layer | Weaver level | Concern | Lib members |
   152	|-------|--------------|---------|-------------|
   153	| **record** | A — technical | What happened: accuracy of capture, storage, replay | atoms, lang, engine, store, migrate |
   154	| **view** | A — technical | Faithful presentation of what happened | painted (external repo) |
   155	| **surfacing** | C — effectiveness | Conduct and authority: host orchestration, coordination, attestation | sign, custody, sdk |
   156	| **relevance** | B — semantic | What matters: judgment over the record | *deliberately none — see below* |
   157	
   158	A layer is a **designation, not a package** — the record layer is five
   159	libraries, not one. Membership lives in this table and in the import DAG
   160	(`tests/test_architecture.py`, Rule 11); a new library must declare its
   161	layer or the suite fails. Apps take no layer assignment — they are
   162	compositions by construction (the loops CLI hosts record reads through view
   163	lenses under surfacing conduct). App-side surfacing occupants (the TUI
   164	shell, cross-implementation protocol artifacts) belong to the layer by role
   165	without being libs.
   166	
   167	**Why the record layer can make correctness claims:** it keeps Shannon's
   168	discipline of excluding semantics. FTS matches, it does not rank. Folds
   169	project, they do not judge. Accuracy is provable in the record layer
   170	precisely because meaning is out of scope there.
   171	
   172	**Surfacing is relational, not correct.** Host shells, protocol
   173	coordination, signing — these are reliable-or-not, conducted-well-or-not;
   174	they make claims about conduct and authority, not about truth. The layer
   175	exists as a *name* because its absence caused misfilings: with only record
